# =============================================================================
# Snort 3 local rules — detection for the 6 Mininet attack types
# =============================================================================
# These are the ONLY attacks generated by SDN Topology/topology.py:
#   1. ICMP Flood              hping3 --icmp --flood
#   2. SYN Flood               hping3 -S --flood -p 80
#   3. UDP Flood               hping3 --udp --flood -p 53
#   4. Port Scan               nmap -sS -p 1-1000
#   5. ARP Spoofing            arpspoof
#   6. Control Plane Saturation  hping3 --udp --flood -p ++1
#
# Each rule's msg maps to a CANONICAL class via snort_monitor.classify_attack()
# (ICMP Flood / SYN Flood / UDP Flood / Port Scan / ARP Spoofing /
# Control Plane Saturation), so the controller labels + blocks correctly.
#
# Port Scan (5) and ARP Spoofing (4) are handled by the port_scan and arp_spoof
# INSPECTORS in sdn_ips.lua, not by rate rules here — see the notes at the end
# for why, plus a rule-based fallback you can enable if an inspector won't load.
#
# Thresholds are tuned for this testbed's TAP/mirror (Snort sees the mirrored
# copy). A real flood is thousands of pkts/sec, far above these counts; the
# values are set high enough to clear the benign bursts (pingall, IoT telemetry)
# and nmap's ~1000-SYN total. Re-tune on the VM if needed.
# =============================================================================

# 1. ICMP Flood — many ICMP echo-requests (itype 8) from one source.
alert icmp any any -> any any (msg:"ICMP Flood"; itype:8; detection_filter:track by_src, count 500, seconds 5; sid:1000001; rev:3;)

# 2. SYN Flood — high-rate TCP SYNs from one source. Count is set well ABOVE
#    nmap's ~1000-SYN total so a port scan does NOT trip this rule (the scan is
#    caught by the port_scan inspector instead) — clean SYN-flood vs scan split.
alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"SYN Flood"; flags:S; detection_filter:track by_src, count 2000, seconds 5; sid:1000002; rev:1;)

# 3. UDP Flood — hping3 --udp --flood -p 53 (fixed DNS port).
alert udp any any -> any 53 (msg:"UDP Flood"; detection_filter:track by_src, count 500, seconds 5; sid:1000003; rev:1;)

# 6. Control Plane Saturation — hping3 --udp --flood -p ++1 sprays INCREMENTING
#    destination ports, so it targets mostly NON-53 ports (the signature is that
#    port spread). This is what separates CPS from the UDP Flood rule above.
alert udp any any -> any !53 (msg:"Control Plane Saturation"; detection_filter:track by_src, count 500, seconds 5; sid:1000004; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Port Scan  -> handled by the `port_scan` inspector in sdn_ips.lua (GID 122).
#    Why an inspector and not a rule: nmap -sS sends ~1 SYN to MANY ports; a rate
#    rule on `flags:S` cannot tell that apart from a SYN flood (both are just
#    SYNs). The port_scan inspector keys on DESTINATION-PORT DIVERSITY, so it
#    fires on the scan and NOT on a single-port flood.
#
#    FALLBACK: if the port_scan inspector fails `snort -c sdn_ips.lua -T` on your
#    build, comment it out in the .lua and uncomment the rule below. NOTE: this
#    rule overlaps with the SYN Flood rule at high rates — keep the SYN-flood
#    count (2000) well above it, and prefer running attacks one type at a time.
# alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"Port Scan"; flags:S; detection_filter:track by_src, count 30, seconds 5; sid:1000005; rev:1;)
#
# 5. ARP Spoofing -> handled by the `arp_spoof` inspector in sdn_ips.lua
#    (GID 112). arpspoof is low-rate (~1 reply/2s), so a rate rule can't catch it
#    reliably; the inspector detects the IP->MAC binding conflict directly. The
#    controller's DAI tier (traffic_capture.py) remains the authoritative ARP
#    detector for this project; the inspector is Snort-side corroboration.
# =============================================================================
